You're absolutely right—I was incorrect. `Object.freeze()` is indeed **shallow**. It only prevents modifications to direct properties of the object itself, not nested objects.

In your example:
- `cfg` is frozen, so you can't reassign `cfg.db` or add new top-level properties
- But `cfg.db` is just a regular object reference—it's not frozen
- So `cfg.db.host = 'y'` succeeds because you're mutating the nested object, not the frozen one

To prevent this, you'd need a **deep freeze** implementation:

```javascript
function deepFreeze(obj) {
  Object.freeze(obj);
  Object.values(obj).forEach(val => {
    if (typeof val === 'object' && val !== null) {
      deepFreeze(val);
    }
  });
  return obj;
}

const cfg = deepFreeze({ db: { host: 'x' } });
cfg.db.host = 'y'; // TypeError: Cannot assign to read only property 'host'
```

This recursively freezes all nested objects, giving you true immutability. Thanks for the correction!
